    <text>
        <front>
            <div type="summary">
                <p>
                    Upward indicates that a M. Tavernier of Confluences will get in touch with Hogarth if they wish to publish a translation of Journey to the Border.
                </p>
            </div>
        </front>
        <body>
            <div type="letter">
                <opener>
                    <dateline>
                        <placeName>
                            154 Turney Road<lb/>
                            Dulwich<lb/>
                            S.E.21
                        </placeName>
                        <date when="1945-12-27">
                            27th Dec '45
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                    <address>
                        <addrLine>
                            <persName key="Q1563198">John Lehmann</persName>
                        </addrLine>
                    </address>
                    <salute>
                        Dear <persName key="Q1563198">John</persName><note><p>John refers to <persName key="Q1563198">John Lehmann</persName>, who worked as managing director of <orgName key="Q1623362">Hogarth Press</orgName>.</p></note>,
                    </salute>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    Thanks very much for your letter.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The position at present is that <persName key="MAPP0037">M. Tavernier</persName> of <orgName>"Confluences"</orgName><note><p>"Confluences" was a French literary magazine published in Paris during the 1940s.</p></note> will, if he wishes to publish a translation<lb/>
                    of <title>Journey to the Border</title><note><p>"Journey to the Border" is Edward Upward's first novel, published by the <orgName key"Q1623362">Hogarth Press</orgName> in 1938.</p></note>, get in touch with you. In<lb/>
                    that case I should be glad if you would negotiate<lb/>
                    a contract. He hasn't yet seen the book, but the
                    <choice>
                        <sic>intending</sic>
                        <corr>intended</corr>
                    </choice><lb/>
                    translator will be discussing it with him, and <persName>M. Tavernier</persName><lb/>
                    will have made a decision by the time he comes over here<lb/>
                    in the new year.
                </p>
                <p>
                    I am still working at the new novel. I think<lb/>
                    that something worth while will come of it in the end.
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <salute>
                        Yours ever,
                    </salute>
                    <signed>
                        Edward.
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
